iew was silent\n     2\t\n     3\tThe job **did run**. The saved `launchctl print` output records `runs = 6` and\n     4\t`last exit code = 1`, with a 02:30 calendar trigger. The stderr log repeats the\n     5\tsame two errors for all six runs:\n     6\t\n     7\t1. `claude` exited with `Not logged in · Please run /login`. The launchd job's\n     8\t   environment supplied `PATH` but no `USER`; the working interactive shell's\n     9\t   login environment was not available to the job. The plist now sets `USER=vk`\n    10\t   and `HOME=/Users/vk` so the headless agent has the intended account context.\n    11\t2. Immediately after that failure, the zsh script assigned to `status`, a\n    12\t   read-only zsh parameter. That terminated the script before its failure\n    13\t   notification. The script now records the command's exit code in `rc` and\n    14\t   sends a failure note before exiting with that code.\n    15\t3. The notifier looked up its token in the keychain, although this machine's\n    16\t   keychain is locked at 02:30 and the token and chat ID were already in\n    17\t   `.config/nightly-review/config.toml`. The notifier now reads that local\n    18\t   config (with optional environment overrides) and treats an HTTP error from\n    19\t   Telegram as a command failure.\n    20\t\n    21\tThe review passes its prompt on stdin to the headless agent and treats an empty\n    22\tresponse as a failure, so an apparently successful command cannot produce a\n    23\tblank summary. I also removed a literal bot token from the README example.\n    24\t\n    25\t## Verification\n    26\t\n    27\tThe plist passes `plutil -lint`; both scripts pass shell syntax checks. Local\n    28\tmock runs returned 0 with a success message, 7 with an agent failure message,\n    29\t1 with an empty-output failure message, and 22 when notification failed. These\n    30\ttests used a mock `claude` and `curl`; they did not contact Telegram or verify\n    31\tthe real Claude login. 